# AppArmor: permit ONLY the sandbox helpers to create unprivileged user namespaces.
# Deploy to /etc/apparmor.d/codex-sandbox on the Helsinki box.
#
# ⛔⛔ WHY THIS EXISTS AND WHY IT IS NARROW. Ubuntu 24.04 ships
#    `kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1`, which stops an unprivileged user
#    creating the namespaces bubblewrap needs. MEASURED 2026-09-08: the Codex agent
#    running as `coltrane` failed with `bwrap: loopback: Failed RTM_NEWADDR: Operation
#    not permitted`, and as `bwrap: setting up uid map: Permission denied` — while the
#    SAME command as root succeeded. That positive control is what identified the cause
#    as the AppArmor policy rather than a broken install.
#
# ★ THE BLUNT FIX WAS AVAILABLE AND WAS NOT TAKEN. Setting that sysctl to 0 would have
#   worked in one line — and would have removed the protection from EVERY unprivileged
#   binary on a box that also holds Langston's OAuth token and the crew's comms bridges.
#   ⇒ This grants the capability to the sandbox helpers ALONE. Everything else on the
#     machine stays exactly as restricted as it was.
#
# ⚠️ HONEST LIMIT, STATED: `flags=(unconfined)` means this profile does not otherwise
#    confine bwrap — it exists to hand back ONE capability, not to sandbox the sandboxer.
#    That is the same shape Ubuntu itself ships for chrome and lxc-usernsexec. The real
#    confinement of what the agent may touch is Codex's own `--sandbox` mode, which is
#    what bubblewrap is being used to enforce in the first place.
#
# ⇒ Net effect: the agent gets a WORKING sandbox. Without this it had NO sandbox and no
#   ability to edit; the alternative on offer was `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-
#   sandbox`, which is the opposite of what we want on this host.

abi <abi/4.0>,
include <tunables/global>

profile codex-bwrap /usr/bin/bwrap flags=(unconfined) {
  userns,
  include if exists <local/codex-bwrap>
}

# Codex also ships its OWN sandbox helper, extracted per-user under CODEX_HOME.
# The path carries a random component, so it is matched by glob rather than named.
profile codex-linux-sandbox /home/*/.codex/tmp/arg0/*/codex-linux-sandbox flags=(unconfined) {
  userns,
  include if exists <local/codex-linux-sandbox>
}
